ok   the entry excuses that mutant and not the other one
ok   an entry of the old line-number form excuses nothing
ok   the same mutant is one line further down after an edit above it
ok   its key does not mention the line number
ok   two identical statements give two mutants with the same text
ok   the two of them are told apart by an occurrence number
ok   an entry with an occurrence number names that one mutant
ok   an entry with the occurrence number 1 names the first mutant
ok   entry_text of every mutant is read back to its own key
ok   an entry without a number matches neither mutant of a group of two
== every entry of an equivalent file matches one mutant (item 11)
ok   an entry that names one mutant passes
ok   an entry that names no mutant fails and is named
ok   the same entry twice fails
ok   an entry of the old line-number form fails and is named
ok   a line that is not an entry fails
ok   --keys prints an entry for every mutant, and each reads back to the key of its mutant
== the report of a compile error (item 6)
